<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Protest and freedom · 1890 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">A Portuguesa</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Portugal: a protest march of 1890 that became the anthem of the new Portuguese Republic in 1911.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Henrique Lopes de Mendonça</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Alfredo Keil</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Portuguese; the republican movement; the Portuguese Republic</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Lisbon, Portugal</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1890; national anthem since 1911; official version approved 16 July 1957</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Henrique Lopes de Mendonça, music by Alfredo Keil, 1890)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>On 11 January 1890 the United Kingdom issued an ultimatum demanding that Portugal give up colonising the land between its colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, on the east, which would have joined them into one territory, as proposed on the Rose-coloured Map. Despite a popular uproar, the Portuguese government accepted the demands. The surrender added to the unpopularity of King Carlos I and the monarchy and won support for the growing republican movement.</p><p>The night after the ultimatum was accepted, the composer Alfredo Keil, at the suggestion of a group of friends that included Rafael Bordalo Pinheiro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teofilo-braga">Teófilo Braga</a>, wrote the melody of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-portuguesa">A Portuguesa</a>&quot; as a patriotic protest march, and the writer Henrique Lopes de Mendonça agreed to Keil's request for words to fit it. Mendonça said it was a song &quot;in which the fatherland's wounded soul would merge with its ambitions of freedom and revival&quot;, and he hoped the people would take it up as an anthem of their longing for national vindication. The march spread quickly: several thousand copies of the sheet music were handed out free, with fliers and posters, and verses were translated into other languages. On 29 March 1890 it was performed at the Great Patriotic Concert at the Teatro Nacional de São Carlos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, and at every other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in the capital; canned sardines and cookies were even named after it.</p><p>The song was soon seen as a political weapon and turned into a republican hymn, and both authors felt obliged to disown that reading and stress that it belonged to no party. On 31 January 1891 republicans rebelled in the northern city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> and adopted &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-portuguesa">A Portuguesa</a>&quot; as their marching song; the revolt was crushed and the song was banned. It was not forgotten: on 5 October 1910 a new and stronger rebellion broke out with &quot;A Portuguesa&quot; playing in the background, and a year later the first session of the Constituent Assembly proclaimed it the national anthem of the Republic, replacing &quot;Hino da Carta&quot;, the anthem of the deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>.</p><p>As different versions of the melody came into use, the government set up a committee in 1956 to fix an official one; the present version was proposed on 16 July 1957 and approved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union">Council of Ministers</a>. The official anthem consists of the first verse and the chorus of Mendonça's poem. It is played at civilian and military ceremonies honouring the country, the flag or the President of the Republic, at receptions for foreign heads of state after the visitor's anthem, and during the President's official visits abroad.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to the Portuguese to rise again and restore the nation's past greatness: it invokes the heroes of the sea and the voices of the ancestors, and its chorus summons the people to arms in defence of the homeland. The mood is martial and defiant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Portuguese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Portuguese</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Heróis do mar, nobre povo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Heroes of the sea, noble people,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Nação valente, imortal</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>valiant, immortal nation,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Levantai hoje de novo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>raise up today once more</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">O esplendor de Portugal!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the splendour of Portugal!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Entre as brumas da memória,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Among the mists of memory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Ó Pátria, sente-se a voz</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Homeland, is felt the voice</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Dos teus egrégios avós</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of your illustrious forefathers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Que há-de guiar-te à vitória!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that shall guide you to victory!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Às armas, às armas!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, to arms!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Sobre a terra sobre o mar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Over the land, over the sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Às armas, às armas!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to arms, to arms!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Pela Pátria lutar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To fight for the Homeland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Contra os canhões marchar, marchar!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>against the cannons march, march!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Desfralda a invicta Bandeira,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Unfurl the unconquered Flag</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">À luz viva do teu céu!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in the living light of your sky!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Brade a Europa à terra inteira:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let Europe cry out to the whole earth:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Portugal não pereceu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Portugal has not perished</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Beija o solo teu jucundo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your joyful soil is kissed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">O oceano, a rugir d`amor,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by the ocean, roaring with love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">E o teu Braço vencedor</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and your victorious Arm</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Deu mundos novos ao mundo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>gave new worlds to the world!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Às armas, às armas!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, to arms!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Sobre a terra sobre o mar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Over the land, over the sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Às armas, às armas!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to arms, to arms!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Pela Pátria lutar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To fight for the Homeland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Contra os canhões marchar, marchar!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>against the cannons march, march!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Saudai o Sol que desponta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Salute the Sun that is rising</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Sobre um ridente porvir;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>over a smiling future;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Seja o eco de uma afronta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let the echo of an affront</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">O sinal de ressurgir.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>be the signal to rise again.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Raios dessa aurora forte</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rays of that strong dawn</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">São como beijos de mãe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>are like a mother's kisses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Que nos guardam, nos sustêm,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>which guard us, sustain us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Contra as injúrias da sorte.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>against the injuries of fortune.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Às armas, às armas!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, to arms!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Sobre a terra, sobre o mar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Over the land, over the sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Às armas, às armas!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to arms, to arms!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Pela Pátria lutar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To fight for the Homeland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Contra os canhões marchar, marchar!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>against the cannons march, march!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Henrique Lopes de Mendonça (1890). Text from <a href="https://pt.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Portuguesa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 2.2: 'sente-se a voz' is 'the voice is felt' or 'one hears the voice'.</li><li>line 2.3: 'avós' is literally 'grandparents', here 'forefathers, ancestors'; 'egrégios' is 'illustrious, eminent'.</li><li>line 4.1: 'Desfralda' is addressed to the Homeland ('unfurl', singular).</li><li>lines 5.1-5.2: the original order is 'Kisses your joyful soil / the ocean, roaring with love': the ocean is the subject. 'jucundo' is 'joyful, pleasant'.</li><li>line 7.3: 'afronta' is an affront or insult: the echo of an insult is to be the signal of rising again.</li><li>line 8.4: 'sorte' is 'fortune, fate'.</li></ul></details></section>
